title: "Benchmarking GNN Inference on the Intel Core Ultra NPU: A Latency, Quantization, and Energy Analysis"
abstract: >
  Neural Processing Units (NPUs) in consumer processors such as Intel Core Ultra (Meteor Lake)
  promise energy-efficient inference at the edge. This paper measures 14 models—Graph Neural Networks
  (GNNs), convolutional networks, and vision transformers—on the CPU, integrated GPU (iGPU), and NPU
  of a Core Ultra 5 125H platform running OpenVINO, across three Open Graph Benchmark datasets
  (ogbn-arxiv, ogbn-proteins, ogbn-products). The NPU delivers strong FP32 performance for dense vision
  models (MobileNetV2: 1.90 ms, ResNet50: 3.94 ms, ViT-Tiny: 9.10 ms, 4.5-11.4x over CPU), but offers
  negligible to negative returns for GNN workloads—the iGPU consistently delivers the lowest GNN
  latency across all tested architectures. For most GNNs, NPU latency remains within 6% of CPU; SGC
  INT8 exhibits a 2.2x regression over FP32 (173.90 ms vs. 78.59 ms), MPNN falls back entirely to CPU,
  and three architectures (GAT, GATv2, EfficientNet-B0) fail INT8 compilation. NPU power isolation
  was unavailable through the SoCWatch PMT interface; CPU and GPU measurements show comparable package
  power (9.1-12.5 W), with INT8 offering at best 18% energy reduction on CPU. These findings indicate
  that while the Meteor Lake NPU is a capable accelerator for dense vision inference, the current
  OpenVINO toolchain and hardware architecture are not yet mature for sparse graph workloads—particularly
  under quantization. The benchmarking suite and processed artifacts are released at
  https://yusufarbc.github.io/intel-npu-gnn-benchmarking/.
